class.description:
Thousands of rows that have no node of their own travel as whole columns here rather than as one message each. [NetwSchema] declares the columns and [method Netw.table] opens the table. [codeblock] class Mobs: static var schema := Netw.configure_schema(&"Mob") static var pos := schema.vector3(&"pos") static var hp := schema.u16(&"hp")
@onready var mobs := Netw.table(self, &"Mob") [/codeblock] One session hands out one handle per table. Connect [signal received] once and keep the handle in a field. [b]Writing[/b] Every column the schema declares is staged each wave, and every staged array holds one element per route. [method commit] stamps the wave with the session's tick. [codeblock] func _on_tick() -> void: mobs.write_routes(routes) mobs.write_column(Mobs.pos, positions) mobs.write_column(Mobs.hp, health) mobs.commit() [/codeblock] Any peer commits its own rows. Only the peer holding authority sends them to the others, so a commit off authority stays local. [b]Reading[/b] A column comes back as the packed array it was written as, in row order. [codeblock] func _ready() -> void: mobs.received.connect(_on_mobs)
func _on_mobs(_tick: int) -> void: var pos: PackedVector3Array = mobs.read_column(Mobs.pos) multimesh.instance_count = pos.size() for row in pos.size(): multimesh.set_instance_transform(row, Transform3D(Basis(), pos[row])) [/codeblock] [method read_births] and [method read_deaths] name the routes the latest wave added and removed. A reader that keeps an object per row steps them from those two rather than diffing the table. [b]Saving[/b] [method flush] writes the committed rows into a [NetwDatabase] as one record and [method hydrate] loads them back under fresh routes. Both settle through a [NetwPromise].
